CH = dict(num=7, title="India's Cultural Roots", tag="History", theme="Vedas, Buddhism, Jainism, folk and tribal traditions",
 intro="Indian culture is like an ancient tree: many roots nourish a common trunk from which many branches (art, literature, science, medicine, religion, governance and 'schools of thought') grow. This chapter studies the Vedas and Vedic culture, the Upaniṣhads, the rise of Buddhism and Jainism, and the contribution of folk and tribal traditions.",
 big_questions=["What are the Vedas? What is their message?", "What new schools of thought emerged in India in the 1st millennium BCE? What are their core principles?", "What is the contribution of folk and tribal traditions to Indian culture?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "7.1 The Tree of Indian Culture"),
 ("ul", ["Indian culture is several millenniums old; some roots go back to the **Indus-Sarasvatī civilisation**.",
  "A **school of thought** = a group of thinkers or spiritual seekers who share similar ideas about human life and the world. Hundreds emerged over time.",
  "**Spiritual** = concerned with the spirit or soul (*ātman*); **seeker** = one who seeks truth (sage, saint, yogi, philosopher).",
  "The text avoids calling Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism and Sikhism just 'religions' because each has philosophical, spiritual, religious, ethical and social aspects; 'religion' is too limiting."]),
 ("h2", "7.2 The Vedas and Vedic Culture"),
 ("h3", "What are the Vedas?"),
 ("ul", ["*Veda* comes from Sanskrit **vid** = knowledge (cf. *vidyā*). There are **four Vedas**: **Ṛig Veda, Yajur Veda, Sāma Veda, Atharva Veda** — India's most ancient texts and among the oldest in the world.",
  "They contain thousands of **hymns** (prayers as poems and songs), **recited orally**, composed in the **Sapta Sindhava** region. The Ṛig Veda is the oldest; scholars place it between the **5th and 2nd millennium BCE**.",
  "Memorised through rigorous training for **100–200 generations** and passed on almost unchanged. In **2008 UNESCO** recognised Vedic chanting as a 'masterpiece of the oral and intangible heritage of humanity'.",
  "Hymns were composed by **rishis** (male seers) and **rishikas** (female seers) in early Sanskrit, addressed to deities such as **Indra, Agni, Varuṇa, Mitra, Sarasvatī, Uṣhas**.",
  "Deities with the seers sustained **ṛitam** — truth and order in human life and the **cosmos**.",
  "*Ekam sat viprā bahudhā vadanti* — 'The Existent is one, but sages give it many names' (the gods are one reality).",
  "Key values: **Truth** (often another name for God). The last mantras of the Ṛig Veda call for **unity**: 'Come together, speak together; common be your mind'",
  "**Rituals (yajña):** offerings and prayers to deities, daily ones to **Agni** (fire), becoming more complex over time."]),
 ("h3", "Vedic society"),
 ("ul", ["Organised in **janas** (clans); the Ṛig Veda lists over 30 — **Bharatas, Purus, Kurus, Yadus, Turvaśhas** — each linked with a region of the north-west.",
  "Clues about governance: **rājā** (king), **sabhā** and **samiti** (assemblies).",
  "Professions: agriculturist, weaver, potter, builder, carpenter, healer, dancer, barber, priest."]),
 ("h3", "Vedic schools of thought"),
 ("ul", ["The **Upaniṣhads** built on the Vedas and introduced **rebirth** and **karma** (actions and their results).",
  "**Vedanta:** everything — human life, nature, universe — is one divine essence, **brahman** (not the god Brahmā), sometimes called **tat** ('that'). Mantras: *aham brahmāsmi* ('I am brahman') and *tat tvam asi* ('You are That').",
  "**Ātman** (Self) — divine essence in every being, ultimately one with brahman; so all things are connected. Prayer: *sarve bhavantu sukhinah* — 'May all creatures be happy'.",
  "**Yoga** (early 1st millennium BCE) developed methods to realise brahman in one's consciousness. Vedanta, Yoga and others form the foundations of what is today called **Hinduism**."]),
 ("table", ["Upaniṣhad story", "Message"], [
  ["**Śhvetaketu and Uddālaka** (Chhāndogya)", "Proud after 12 years in a gurukula, he cannot answer his father. The father explains brahman using the seed of the banyan fruit and clay pots: all things emerge from one essence — 'You are That'"],
  ["**Nachiketa and Yama** (Katha)", "Given to Yama (god of death) by an angry father; persists in asking what happens after death. Yama teaches the ātman is hidden in all, neither born nor dies"],
  ["**Gārgī and Yājñavalkya** (Bṛihadāraṇyaka)", "At King Janaka's debate the rishika Gārgī questions the rishi Yājñavalkya on the nature of the world and brahman; resumes later and gets the explanation. Questioning is valued, by women, men and children"]]),
 ("h2", "7.3 Buddhism"),
 ("p", "Schools that did not accept the authority of the Vedas also emerged. Buddhism is one."),
 ("ul", ["**Siddhārtha Gautama** born about two-and-a-half millenniums ago (taken as **560 BCE**) in **Lumbini** (today Nepal) into a royal family; grew up protected in a palace.",
  "At **29** he saw an old man, a sick man, a dead body and a happy, peaceful **ascetic**; he left home, wife and son.",
  "After meditating under a **pipal tree at Bodh Gaya (Bihar)** he attained **enlightenment**: **avidyā (ignorance) and attachment** cause suffering; he devised a method to remove them. He became the **Buddha** ('enlightened/awakened one').",
  "Teachings: **ahimsa** (non-hurting; includes thought), inner discipline. 'Not by water is one made pure; conquering oneself is greater than conquering a thousand men on the battlefield a thousand times.'",
  "Founded the **Sangha** — community of **bhikṣhus** (monks) and later **bhikṣhuṇīs** (nuns). Influence spread over all Asia.",
  "**Jātaka tales:** stories of the Buddha's former births, e.g. the monkey-king who made his body a bridge for his troop to cross a stream, dying in the process (carved at Bharhut, Madhya Pradesh)."]),
 ("h2", "7.4 Jainism"),
 ("ul", ["Widespread at the same time as Buddhism, though roots are said to be far older.",
  "**Prince Vardhamāna**, born early 6th century BCE near **Vaiśhālī (Bihar)**; left home at **30**; after **12 years** of ascetic discipline achieved supreme wisdom; known as **Mahāvīra** ('great hero').",
  "*Jain* from **jina** = 'conqueror' — of ignorance and attachment, not of territory.",
  "Core ideas: **ahimsa**, **anekāntavāda** ('not just one' view — truth has many aspects) and **aparigraha** (non-possession, limiting oneself to necessities); interdependence of all beings, even invisible organisms.",
  "Story of **Rohineya**, the burglar who heard a few lines of Mahāvīra's sermon, later confessed, returned the treasure and became a monk — right action and thinking and a second chance."]),
 ("p", "Monks and nuns of both faiths travelled widely, founded **monasteries** and lived in **rock-cut caves** (e.g. **Ellora, Maharashtra**, 6th–10th centuries CE, with Hindu, Buddhist and Jain caves). The **Chārvāka/Lokāyata** school held only the material world exists and was not popular — showing diversity of belief."),
 ("table", ["", "Vedic/Vedanta", "Buddhism", "Jainism"], [
  ["Founder / source", "Vedas, Upaniṣhads", "Siddhārtha Gautama (Buddha)", "Mahāvīra (Vardhamāna)"],
  ["Birthplace", "—", "Lumbini (Nepal)", "Near Vaiśhālī (Bihar)"],
  ["Enlightenment", "Realising brahman / ātman", "Bodh Gaya, under pipal tree", "After 12 years of asceticism"],
  ["Key ideas", "Brahman, ātman, karma, rebirth", "Ignorance and attachment cause suffering; ahimsa", "Ahimsa, anekāntavāda, aparigraha"],
  ["Order", "Gurukula, rishis", "Sangha (bhikṣhus / bhikṣhuṇīs)", "Monks and nuns"]]),
 ("note", "IMPORTANT", "**Shared concepts:** dharma, karma, rebirth, ending ignorance and suffering — the common 'trunk' of the cultural tree."),
 ("h2", "7.5 Folk and Tribal Roots"),
 ("ul", ["**Oral traditions** pass on teachings without written texts; **folk traditions** come from common people, **tribal traditions** from tribes.",
  "A **tribe**: group of families or clans sharing common descent, culture and language, living as a close-knit community under a chief, with no private property. Ancient India had no word for tribe — they were *janas*; the Constitution says 'tribes' / **janjāti**.",
  "**2011:** 705 tribes, ~104 million people (more than Australia and UK together). The 19th-century idea of 'primitive' is now abandoned.",
  "**Shared ideas with Hinduism:** nature (mountains, rivers, trees, animals, stones) is sacred because consciousness is behind it; many deities plus belief in a higher divinity.",
  "Examples: **Toda** (Nilgiris, Tamil Nadu) treat over 30 peaks as divine and avoid pointing at them; **Donyipolo** (Sun and Moon) in Arunachal Pradesh; **Khandoba** in central India; **Singbonga** among Munda and Santhal.",
  "**Exchange both ways:** **Jagannath at Puri** was originally a tribal deity; mother-goddess forms; some tribes have their own versions of the **Mahābhārata and Rāmāyaṇa**.",
  "André Béteille: castes and tribes influenced each other's beliefs; Hinduism too has been shaped by tribal religions — **mutual enrichment**."]),
 ("note", "REMEMBER", "The **banyan tree** (sacred to Hinduism, Buddhism, Jainism) symbolises Indian civilisation: deep roots, massive trunk, branches that throw fresh roots."),
 ("h2", "7.6 Quick Reference"),
 ("qa", [
  ("What does 'Veda' mean and how many Vedas are there?", "Knowledge (from vid); four — Ṛig, Yajur, Sāma and Atharva."),
  ("How were the Vedas preserved?", "By oral memorisation through rigorous training for 100–200 generations; UNESCO recognised Vedic chanting in 2008."),
  ("What is ṛitam?", "Truth and order in human life and the cosmos, sustained by the deities and seers."),
  ("Explain 'Ekam sat viprā bahudhā vadanti'.", "The supreme reality is one; sages call it by many names."),
  ("What were janas, sabhā and samiti?", "Clans of Vedic society; assemblies that were part of governance."),
  ("What new ideas did the Upaniṣhads introduce?", "Rebirth, karma, brahman and ātman, and the oneness of all."),
  ("Who was Nachiketa?", "A boy who sought Yama's teaching about death and learned that the ātman is immortal."),
  ("How did Siddhārtha become the Buddha?", "After seeing old age, sickness and death, he left the palace and attained enlightenment at Bodh Gaya, learning ignorance and attachment cause suffering."),
  ("What are the three key teachings of Jainism?", "Ahimsa, anekāntavāda and aparigraha."),
  ("What does ahimsa mean?", "Non-hurting — avoiding violence in action and in thought."),
  ("Why is 'religion' avoided for these traditions?", "They have philosophical, spiritual, ethical and social aspects as well; religion is too limiting."),
  ("Give examples of exchange between tribal and mainstream traditions.", "Jagannath of Puri from a tribal deity; tribes with their own Mahābhārata and Rāmāyaṇa.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["The **Vedas** are India's oldest texts, orally preserved; their message is the **unity of truth** and of people.",
  "The **Upaniṣhads** introduced **brahman, ātman, karma and rebirth**; Vedanta and Yoga became foundations of Hinduism.",
  "**Buddhism** and **Jainism** questioned Vedic authority and stressed **ahimsa**, discipline and conquering ignorance and attachment.",
  "All shared **dharma, karma, rebirth** and the search to end suffering.",
  "**Folk and tribal traditions** treat nature as sacred and have exchanged ideas freely with other traditions — mutual enrichment."])
